  [38;5;153m[required] }[39m and each rule is [38;5;153m(value) => string | null[39m (message or null). Ships with only the rules login actually
  needs; a form needing [38;5;153mminLength[39m adds it then. Core is ~20 lines. Buys: genuinely reusable, trivially unit-testable, no
  speculative API. Costs: each form declares its own rule array. Rules out: nothing — schema-style sugar can be layered
  on later.

  [1m2. HTML-native constraint validation wrapper[0m — put [38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminlength[39m, [38;5;153mtype="email"[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m on the inputs in markup,
  and the shared module just walks [38;5;153mform.elements[39m collecting [38;5;153mvalidity[39m / [38;5;153mvalidationMessage[39m into the result shape. Buys:
  the shared module never grows as forms are added; new forms get validation by writing attributes. Costs: messages are
  browser-supplied and awkward to customize or translate, and cross-field rules ("passwords match") have no home. Rules
  out: custom messaging without a second mechanism bolted on.

  [1m3. Declarative schema + full rule library[0m — [38;5;153m{ username: { required: true, minLength: 3 }, email: { required: true, [39m
  [38;5;153memail: true } }[39m with a built-in library of rule types. Buys: most expressive, most familiar. Costs: you write and
  maintain a rule library for one form's worth of real requirements. Rules out: little, but it's the most code with the
  least evidence behind it.

  [1mMy recommendation: #1.[0m With no second form in existence, the risk here is inventing an API against imagined consumers.
  #1 is the smallest thing that is honestly reusable — the core evaluator is the reusable part, and the rule vocabulary
  grows only when a real form demands it. #3 is #1 plus a library you can add later without changing the core. I'd skip
  #2 unless custom error messages genuinely don't matter to you, because that limitation shows up the first time a
  designer asks for wording.
